    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's not really about feelings though, it's more like... facts. like i can remember the exact color of a car that drove by three years ago or the specific phrasing of a news report from a decade ago. it feels less like a memory and more like a massive database that's just running in the background. i feel like if i don't find a way to archive it properly, something really important is going to be lost forever. can we try to create a system for categorizing these things? something more robust than just a journal?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood—it sounds like you're dealing with a lot of sensory and factual data in your mind that's overwhelming. Creating a structured system could be beneficial. You might want to start by creating categories based on different types of information, such as vehicles, news reports, and so on. Within each category, you can use subcategories for further organization. For example, under 'vehicles,' you could have years, colors, car types, etc. You can digitize your notes, using spreadsheet software, where each cell represents a category and subcategory for tidier and more accessible storage. Database software might be a very comprehensive solution for your needs, allowing you to add details, search through records, and back up all your information securely. This will make sure nothing is lost and you can easily retrieve any piece of data you need. Plus, consistent record-keeping will make it less mentally taxing for you.",
